{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B044",
        "laureates": [
          "David Huang",
          "Eric A. Swanson",
          "James G. Fujimoto"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention of optical coherence tomography"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B038",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B044 remains the most compelling completed invention in this discussion: it made non-invasive, high-resolution imaging of living tissue experimentally practical, and its mature clinical use demonstrates a sustained benefit to humanity rather than a prospective promise. The proposed trio is tied to the seminal demonstration and instrumental realization. This is precisely the sort of original experiment and invention whose scientific and practical consequences can be directly seen.",
    "case_against": "The decisive reservation remains historical rather than scientific. We must distinguish the invention of OCT from prior low-coherence interferometry and establish the exact division of original credit among Huang, Swanson, and Fujimoto. The present exchange provides no archival evidence resolving that question, so the citation must remain narrow and the committee should not confuse subsequent clinical translation with the original invention.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors optical lattice clocks because their magic-wavelength principle, exceptional reproducibility, and compact attribution to Katori and Ye form a mature instrumental advance.",
        "response": "I agree that B038 is unusually strong on reproducibility and credit, and it is my first alternative. However, its own advocates acknowledge that its most distinctive fundamental-physics consequences are still developing. OCT has already opened a durable observational capability in living tissue with demonstrated clinical consequences. B038 could supersede it only if our historical review leaves the OCT invention boundary unresolved."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors the mature explanatory framework of defect-mediated two-dimensional melting, while cautioning that real systems do not always exhibit the ideal transition sequence.",
        "response": "I accept the depth and lasting importance of B007. Its qualification illustrates why maturity cannot be reduced to the elegance of a framework: the scope of the claimed mechanism must correspond exactly to what experiments establish. For B044, the observational and practical result is unusually direct; the comparable task is to determine whether the original OCT experiment, rather than its enabling interferometry or later applications, can be delimited with equal precision."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that optical lattice clocks have a clean two-person attribution but that their citation must be separated from the broader history of optical-frequency standards.",
        "response": "I agree with his insistence on a narrow historical boundary. It reinforces rather than weakens my view of OCT: neither a splendid apparatus nor broad impact is enough without identifying the original invention. The B044 trio remains defensible only for the specific tissue-imaging invention, and not for all low-coherence interferometry or all later ophthalmic applications."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival evidence can cleanly distinguish the original OCT invention from prior low-coherence interferometry.",
      "Whether the three proposed laureates' respective original contributions support their joint credit within the three-person limit.",
      "Whether the committee judges B038's established measurement regime sufficient without awaiting a uniquely decisive fundamental-physics discovery."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent. Göran Johansson is a Chalmers colleague and Bernhard Mehlig works in neighbouring Gothenburg physics, but no connection to the proposed OCT laureates is identified."
  }
}
